PRETABLE -- #531 ip30abortkill, kill table 1 (21-row instrument)
Written 2026-09-17 BEFORE table 1 runs; this file's own mtime is the order discriminator.
Mandatory per the pre-table block: this round ships a kill-table-backed detector.

================================================================================
WHY THIS TABLE EXISTS, STATED PLAINLY
================================================================================
The 21-row instrument reads 21/21 post-fix and 8/21 pre-fix.  THAT DISCHARGES NOTHING
ABOUT THE FLOOR.  Two of the four channel rows added this round -- `isr clr' and
`isr drop 30' -- are RED PRE-FIX ONLY BECAUSE THEIR SEEDING WRITE ABORTS, so they read
`died' rather than a wrong value.  The failability rule is explicit: an accept-side row
is NOT discharged by existing, it is discharged by a MEASURED KILL.  So the L2 floor
breach the panel found at 0x10028 is OPEN until this table runs.

================================================================================
1. SIBLING PARITY -- each de-fatalised arm against its nearest sibling
================================================================================
  ARM                     ANNOUNCE   STORE KEPT   VALUE ROW PINNING THE STORE
  0x10020 WRITE (set)     yes        yes          `isr keep'      (0x5a5a round-trip)
  0x10028 WRITE (clear)   yes        yes          `isr clr'       (0x5050)   <- ADDED
  0x10030 WRITE (ro)      yes        NO (dropped) `isr drop 30'   (0x5a5a)   <- ADDED
  0x10020 READ            yes        n/a          -- decline, no value to pin
  0x10028 READ            yes        n/a          -- decline, no value to pin
  DEVICE_TICK             yes        counter runs `acc 20000'     (multiple of 1000)

  ASYMMETRY FOUND AND CLOSED: before this round, 0x10020's store was pinned by
  `isr keep' and 0x10028's store was pinned by NOTHING, while the two arms are
  otherwise identical in shape.  That asymmetry IS the L2 breach, and four seats
  reached it independently.  0x10030 is asymmetric BY DESIGN (heart.h: read-only,
  so the store is deliberately dropped) -- which is why it owes a row asserting the
  ABSENCE of an effect rather than its presence.

================================================================================
2. CLAIM -> ROW.  Every added comment sentence asserting an observable consequence.
   The trigger is SEMANTIC ("does a row could observe this?"), not a verb list.
================================================================================
  CLAIM (added comment)                                   ROW / OWED SENTENCE
  "the counter update above still runs"                   `acc 20000'
  "odata stays 0 and the tail returns it"                 OWED -- no row reads the
                                                          0x10020 READ value.  Named
                                                          here rather than rowed; see 5.
  "the clear-on-write is DROPPED"                         `isr drop 30'
  "the store is kept" (0x10020)                           `isr keep'
  "the store is kept" (0x10028)                           `isr clr'
  "latch granularity matches the marker's qualification"  NO ROW, AND SAY SO: this is a
                                                          claim about a STRING and a BIT,
                                                          not about device behaviour.  No
                                                          probe row can observe it.  It is
                                                          pinned by M6 below instead.

================================================================================
3. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD -- where they differ, and what that owes
================================================================================
  ARM        GUARD READS                  BODY READS/WRITES
  tick       d->imask0, d->warned         d->warned, d->reg_0x20000
  0x10020    writeflag, d->warned         d->isr, idata, d->warned
  0x10028    writeflag, d->warned         d->isr, idata, d->warned
  0x10030    writeflag, d->warned         d->isr (read), odata, d->warned

  THEY DIFFER AT d->isr: no guard reads it, every ISR body touches it.  The
  disagreeing state that owes a row is therefore "ISR NON-ZERO WHILE THE LATCH IS
  ALREADY CONSUMED" -- and `isr clr' / `isr drop 30' both build it, because each
  seeds through 0x10020 (consuming IP30_W_ISR) before acting on a second arm.

  AND THE SHARED-BITFIELD QUESTION, which the guard-field grep does NOT ask:
  "who else writes d->warned, and can they set my bit?"  Measured: 6 sites, all
  inside this device, all through the two IP30_W_* macros, none outside.  No
  foreign writer.  This is the check that could not have caught #490's collision,
  recorded so the next reader knows its limit.

================================================================================
4. CHANNEL -- per row, the mutant it kills and the observable that differs.
   A row whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is VACUOUS BY
   CONSTRUCTION, and that is knowable NOW, before the table runs.
================================================================================
  ROW            MUTANT IT KILLS                      OBSERVABLE THAT DIFFERS
  isr clr        M1: delete d->isr &= ~idata (0x10028) readback 0x5a5a vs 0x5050
  isr drop 30    M2: re-insert the clear at 0x10030    readback 0x484a vs 0x5a5a
  tick 50 txt    M3: delete the tick fatal()           announce absent vs present
  kill 30 txt    M4: delete the 0x10030 fatal()        announce absent vs present
  tick 50+49     M5: move the guard 1<<50 -> 1<<49     which bit announces
  (no row)       M6: collapse the two latch bits       SEE BELOW -- THIS IS THE ONE
                                                       I EXPECT TO ESCAPE

  *** THE CHANNEL LINE'S OWN PREDICTION, WRITTEN BEFORE THE TABLE RUNS, BECAUSE THE
  RULE IS ONE DAY OLD IN THIS FORM AND ITS CLAIM IS THAT THIS IS KNOWABLE AT WRITING
  TIME. ***

  M6 (collapse IP30_W_ISR to IP30_W_TICK, i.e. one shared bit) IS PREDICTED TO ESCAPE
  ALL 21 ROWS.  Derivation: every session in this probe is a FRESH PROCESS, so no row
  ever exercises a tick announce and an ISR announce in the SAME device lifetime --
  which is exactly the state a shared bit corrupts.  #490 measured this same mutant
  shape (`M3shared') escaping for the same reason.

  IF M6 ESCAPES, THAT IS A PREDICTED, DISCLOSED GAP AND NOT A SURPRISE: it is
  recorded here, before the run, as the cell this detector does not cover.  The
  question it forces -- whether a fresh-process-per-row probe can EVER see a
  shared-latch defect -- is a design question about the instrument, not a row I can
  add, and it is named as such rather than papered over.

================================================================================
5. OWED SENTENCES (checks discharged N/A, WITH THEIR REASON -- an unrun check and an
   inapplicable one must not render the same)
================================================================================
  * 0x10020/0x10028 READ value: no row asserts odata == 0 on those reads.  The minimax
    seat raised the asymmetry and I kept the announce; the VALUE is unpinned.  NOT
    rowed this round: rows are owed PER ENUMERATED MUTANT, and no mutant in 1..6
    distinguishes it.  DISCLOSED as coverage debt.
  * M6 above: predicted to escape, disclosed, not rowed -- see 4.
  * Latch-macro RENUMBERING (deepseek's mutant): N/A, not a floor cell.  All uses go
    through the macros and no observable differs -- an EQUIVALENT mutant, which is a
    RESULT, not a gap.

================================================================================
6. CONTRACT (clause (e)) -- what this detector is designed to catch
================================================================================
  CATCHES: reinstating any of the four abort() calls; deleting any of the five
  announces; deleting either surviving store; re-inserting the dropped 0x10030 clear;
  moving the tick guard between bits 49 and 50; a read arm that clobbers the ISR.
  DOES NOT CATCH: latch-bit collapse across device lifetimes (M6, predicted above);
  latch-macro renumbering (equivalent); the VALUE returned by a declined read;
  anything in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_ip30_2), which is a different device and is filed
  separately as `ip30asdvasdvnb'.
